# Project documentation — durable repository memory

Permixa’s canonical project memory lives under `/docs`.

`docs/AGENT-HANDOFF.md` is the first file any new AI agent should read.

## Before completing any non-trivial implementation task

1. Inspect whether the change affects documented behavior.
2. Update relevant `/docs` files.
3. Update `/docs/CURRENT-STATE.md`.
4. Update `/docs/AGENT-HANDOFF.md` (keep it concise; link deeper docs).
5. Add/update an ADR under `/docs/decisions` if an architecture/security/public-API/schema/concurrency decision was approved.
6. Update phase history (`/docs/CHANGELOG-PHASES.md` and/or `/docs/phases/*`) if a phase/task milestone changed.
7. Update migrations/schema documentation when relevant.
8. Update test totals only after verification.
9. Never fabricate historical rationale. If WHY is unknown, say so.
10. Never describe planned behavior as implemented.
11. Preserve intentionally rejected/deferred approaches when useful (`DEFERRED-SCOPE.md`, ADRs).
12. Keep AGENT-HANDOFF concise; link to deeper docs instead of duplicating them.

## AGENT-HANDOFF living state

Update `docs/AGENT-HANDOFF.md` when any of these change:

- completed phase / current phase / next approved work
- verified test/build baseline
- critical architecture or security invariant
- major public API
- deferred scope
- important migration/schema state

Do not rewrite the entire handoff after every tiny task — keep it current and short.

## Trivial changes

Formatting/typo-only changes with no behavioral significance do not require documentation updates.

## Feature completeness

A feature is not fully complete if important architecture/security/public behavior changed but `/docs` still describes the old behavior.
